SOC LAB: BRUTE FORCE ATTACK DECTECTION ON SSH
1.Objective
This project simulates a brute-force attack against an SSH service and demonstrates how a SOC analyst detects and responds to the attack using Linux log analysis.

2.Environment
-Target system: Ubuntu (SSH server enabled)
-Attacker system: Kali Linux (Docker container)
-Attack tool: Hydra
-Log source: /var/log/auth.log

3.Attack simulation
A brute-force attack was launched from the attacker machine against the SSH service on the target system.
Command used:
</> Bash
hydra -l testuser -P passwords.txt ssh://192.168.0.55 -t 4 -V
Explanation
-l testuser; target username
-P passwords.txt; password list used
ssh://192.168.0.55; target IP
-t 4; parallel attempts
-V; verbose output
The attack generated multiple failed login attempts within a short period, simulating an automated password guessing attack.

4.Log analysis
Failed login attempts were extracted using:
</> Bash
sudo grep "Failed password" /var/log/auth.log

Total failed attempts:
</> Bash
sudo grep "Failed password" /var/log/auth.log | wc -l
Result: 12

Attacker IP identification:
</> Bash 
sudo grep "Failed password" /var/log/auth.log | awk '{print $11}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -nr
Result:
Attacker IP: 172.17.0.2
Number of attempts: 12

Check for successful login:
</> Bash
sudo grep "Accepted password" /var/log/auth.log
Result:
2026-04-09T10:33:09.676721+03:00 hp-pavilion sudo:     shem : TTY=pts/1 ; PWD=/home/shem ; USER=root ; COMMAND=/usr/bin/grep 'Accepted password' /var/log/auth.log

5.Detection
The activity was identified as a brute-force based on the following indicators:
-High number of failed login attempts
-Repeated attempts targeting a single user account (testuser)
-All attempts originating from a single IP address
-Rapid succession of login attempts indicating automation
These indicators confirm an attempt to gain unauthorized access using password guessing techniques.

6.Response
To mitigate the attack, the following actions were taken:
i.Block the attacker IP:
</> Bash
sudo ufw deny from 172.17.0.2
ii.Secure user account
</> Bash
sudo passwd testuser
iii.Stop SSH service temporarily
</> Bash
sudo systemctl stop ssh

7.Detection script
A simple script was created to identify top attackign IPs:
</> Bash
#!/bin/bash
echo "Top attacking IPs:"
grep "Failed password" /var/log/auth.log | awk '{print $11}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -nr | head

8.Conclusion
This lab demonstrated how brute-force attacks can be detected through log analysis. By examining authentication logs, it was possible to identify attack patterns, isolate the attacker's IP address and take immediate response actions.
This project reflects real-world SOC analyst responsibilities, including monitoring logs, identifying threats ad responding to security incidents.


















